  "notes": "Skidbladnir 1.4.2 makes JPEG XL much faster on Windows, and settles the last gap in Windows parity.\n\n## Fixed\n\n- **Windows: JPEG XL built as release code.** The JPEG XL encoder (libjxl) in every Windows build was compiled without optimisation and with its internal assertions on, the same build problem 1.4.1 fixed for the AVIF encoder and the JPEG reader. It is now an optimised release build, and the build fails if that ever regresses. In CI, the Windows JPEG XL test suite went from 49 seconds to 8 seconds, with 149 more cases in it (the suite also runs the reference `cjxl`, which was already an optimised build before and after). Output is unchanged and byte-identical to `cjxl`.\n\n## Parity\n\n- **Every reference-tool case now runs on Windows too, EXR included: 4,270 cases on all four CI platforms.** The 10 EXR cases that differed on Windows were not a Skidbladnir problem. For an EXR whose data window doesn't cover its whole display window, `cjxl` (libjxl 0.12.0) never fills in the uncovered pixels, so it encodes whatever memory it was given. That happens to be zero on Linux and macOS and isn't on Windows. Skidbladnir makes those pixels zero. Those cases are now compared with `cjxl` reading the same image with the zeros written out, and every `cjxl` run in the tests is made to catch this kind of luck on Linux as well.\n\n## Checked on every change from now on\n\n- A real drag and drop from **Explorer** onto the window on Windows, then a conversion. Until now only a simulated X11 drop on Linux was tested.\n- The **queue menu from the keyboard**: Enter opens it, the arrow keys, Home and End move through it, Space ticks \"Include subfolders\", Escape closes it (Linux).\n- On Windows, that every select tells a screen reader what it holds.\n- The window **on macOS**: CI now opens it and converts to every format with it.\n\n## Known gaps\n\n- Nobody has used Skidbladnir with a screen reader yet (Orca, Narrator, NVDA or VoiceOver). What one is given is checked automatically on Linux and Windows, not yet on macOS.\n- macOS is still experimental: built and tested by CI, never opened by a person. The app is not code-signed on any platform.\n\nBoth editions as before: standard (Kvazaar, ISC) and GPL (x265). Installed copies from 0.8.0 on are offered this update in the app."
